# THE GAME MOTION VECTOR FIELD PASS LOOKS AT A CELL BEFORE IT TAKES AN ATOMIC ON IT (2026-10-10, the own build's stage 3).
#
# WHAT AMD'S CODE DOES. The most costly pass of a generated frame (427 us of 1,697 at Quality's guides, 902 of 2,431 at
# DLAA's) builds the game's vector field by "the highest priority wins": every pixel of the guides hands its packed
# vector to the four pixels its half vector lands between (eight atomic maxima: the field is two images, X and Y), and
# then walks back along its vector and hands a lower-priority "secondary" vector to one pixel a step (two atomic maxima
# a step, each also returning what was there). Measured on this card with one thing changed at a time (the probes of
# stage 3): the eight primary atomics are 210 us of the 427, the secondary walk 163 us; as plain stores the primary
# ones would cost 71.
#
# THE CUT. A maximum that is already there need not be taken. Before each atomic maximum the cell is loaded (a plain
# load), and the atomic is taken only when this thread's value is LARGER than what the load saw.
#   WHY THE FIELD ENDS THE SAME. Inside this pass a cell of the field only ever grows: the setup pass zeroed it, and
#   every write of this pass is an atomic maximum. A value that is not larger than what a load saw is not larger than
#   the cell's final value either, so the atomic it skips could not have changed anything. A value that is larger goes
#   through AMD's own atomic, as before. The final content of both images is the maximum of the same set of values.
#   THE SECONDARY WALK reads "what was there" to count the primary vectors it meets, and stops after the fourth. AMD's
#   code takes that from the atomic's returned value; with the cut it is the atomic's returned value where the atomic is
#   taken, and what the load saw where it is not. Both are a look at a cell that other threads are still writing: which
#   primary vectors a walk already finds there depends, in AMD's own code, on the order the card runs the threads in
#   (docs\reports\fg-3\own-build-2026-10-06\critic-plan.md, check 4: "the game vector field has a race"). So for this
#   half the equality is not an argument, it is a measurement: the judge's whole matrix, every pair, and the same
#   matrix again.
#
# MEASURED: see docs\reports\fg-3\own-build-2026-10-06\stage-3 (the table of cuts).
what the game motion vector field pass (I4) loads a cell before each atomic maximum and takes the atomic only when its value is larger than what is there
passes ffx_frameinterpolation_game_motion_vector_field_pass
wave64 it edits one shader file that both the cs_6_2 and the cs_6_6 (wave64) variants are compiled from; no wave operation; the order a card runs the threads in is that card's, and this was run on one GeForce only
file framegeneration/fsr3/include/gpu/frameinterpolation/ffx_frameinterpolation_game_motion_vector_field.h
--- old
                UpdateGameMotionVectorField(iSamplePos, packedVectorPrimary);
--- new
                // (the mod's cut fi-game-field-test-first: a cell only grows in this pass; the atomic is taken only
                // when this value is larger than what a plain load finds there)
                if (rw_game_motion_vector_field_x[iSamplePos] < packedVectorPrimary.x)
                {
                    InterlockedMax(rw_game_motion_vector_field_x[iSamplePos], packedVectorPrimary.x);
                }
                if (rw_game_motion_vector_field_y[iSamplePos] < packedVectorPrimary.y)
                {
                    InterlockedMax(rw_game_motion_vector_field_y[iSamplePos], packedVectorPrimary.y);
                }
--- end
file framegeneration/fsr3/include/gpu/frameinterpolation/ffx_frameinterpolation_game_motion_vector_field.h
--- old
                    const FfxUInt32 uExistingVectorFieldEntry = UpdateGameMotionVectorFieldEx(iSamplePos, packedVectorSecondary);
--- new
                    // (the mod's cut fi-game-field-test-first: AMD's UpdateGameMotionVectorFieldEx, with the same look first.
                    // "What was there" is the atomic's own answer where the atomic is taken, the load's where it is not)
                    FfxUInt32 uPreviousValueX = rw_game_motion_vector_field_x[iSamplePos];
                    if (uPreviousValueX < packedVectorSecondary.x)
                    {
                        InterlockedMax(rw_game_motion_vector_field_x[iSamplePos], packedVectorSecondary.x, uPreviousValueX);
                    }
                    FfxUInt32 uPreviousValueY = rw_game_motion_vector_field_y[iSamplePos];
                    if (uPreviousValueY < packedVectorSecondary.y)
                    {
                        InterlockedMax(rw_game_motion_vector_field_y[iSamplePos], packedVectorSecondary.y, uPreviousValueY);
                    }
                    const FfxUInt32 uExistingVectorFieldEntry = ffxMax(uPreviousValueX, uPreviousValueY);
--- end
